      "text": "To determine the smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that has the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to consider the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients.\n\nGiven that the coefficients of the polynomial are rational, if a polynomial with rational coefficients has a root that is an irrational number, its conjugate must also be a root. Therefore, the conjugate of \\(1+\\sqrt{2}\\) is \\(1-\\sqrt{2}\\). This means that in order for the polynomial to have rational coefficients, every root of the form \\(n+\\sqrt{n+1}\\) must have its conjugate \\(n-\\sqrt{n+1}\\) also as a root.\n\nSo, for each root of the form \\(n+\\sqrt{n+1}\\), the conjugate \\(n-\\sqrt{n+1}\\) must also be a root. This implies that for every number \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000, both \\(n+\\sqrt{n+1}\\) and \\(n-\\sqrt{n+1}\\) must be roots, making the polynomial of degree twice the number of such numbers.\n\nLet's calculate this step by step. The number of terms given is 1000, so the polynomial must have \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\) roots in total. Therefore, the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is 2000.\n\nLet's verify this using Python code, although it's a straightforward mathematical reasoning problem.\n\n```python\n# The number of terms given is 1000\nnum_terms = 1000\n\n# Since each term \\(n + \\sqrt{n+1}\\) has its conjugate \\(n - \\sqrt{n+1}\\) as a root,\n# the polynomial must have 2000 roots in total (since 2 times the number of terms)\nfinal_degree = 2 * num_terms\n\nprint(final_degree)\n```\n```output\n2000\n```\nThe smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
